# Keeps real data out of your commits. Copy this into your own project, or add
# its contents to the end of a .gitignore you already have.
#
# These rules are about LOCATION, not filenames. A filename cannot tell you
# whether a real person is identifiable, so the folders below are the ones you
# designate, and everything in them stays out. DATA-GUARD.md explains how to
# make those same folders unreadable to the agent, which git cannot do.

# The designated folder, at any depth. Written with a trailing /* rather than a
# bare / because git cannot re-include a file whose parent directory is
# excluded, and the exception below needs to work.
#
# This name means "real data lives here" in every project on this machine,
# because the deny rule in DATA-GUARD.md matches it everywhere. Never use it
# for a code folder: code in a folder called sensitive/ is left out of commits
# and hidden from the agent, with no warning. If you already keep real data
# under another folder name, add that name here and to the deny rule, and keep
# it out of your code too.
sensitive/*
**/sensitive/*

# One exception, listed after the rules it overrides so that it wins. The fake
# extract ships because it is what proves the guard fires, and it is engineered
# to be obviously fake. It is named exactly, with no wildcard, because Macs
# ignore filename case: a wildcard here would also let through any real file
# whose name starts with "fake", in any case.
!sensitive/FAKE-eligibility-extract.csv

# Agent scratch, and git worktrees if an agent makes them here. One folder per
# piece of work under .agents/ is scratch and never the record. Neither belongs
# in history.
.agents/
.worktrees/

# Local environment
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
.DS_Store
node_modules/

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Deliberately NOT enabled, because they would bite a normal project.
#
# A blunt rule against anything data-shaped is good defence where no such file
# is ever legitimate, and quietly destructive where one is. A project with test
# fixtures in CSV, or a data/ folder of public reference tables, would find them
# silently uncommitted and hard to diagnose.
#
# So: uncomment any line below that is true of YOUR project, and leave the rest.
# If you do uncomment the CSV line, add an exception for any fixture you need,
# the way the fake extract above is handled.
#
# *.csv
# *.xlsx
# *.xls
# *.mdb
# *.accdb
# data/
# extracts/
# *-internal.md
# *-confidential.md
# *-transcript.md

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# HourKeep. Everything above is the bootstrap starter's .gitignore, unchanged.
# Below are the rules carried over from v1's .gitignore that still apply, plus
# the Vite build output. v1's Next.js rules (.next/, out/, next-env.d.ts) went
# with v1; see docs/decisions/0001-set-aside-v1-in-the-same-repository.md.

# Build output and test scratch. Anchored to the repository root with a leading
# slash, as v1 had them: unanchored, coverage/ would also hide a source folder
# named after the Medicaid word.
/dist/
/coverage/
/test-results/
/playwright-report/
.playwright-mcp/
*.tsbuildinfo

# Private keys, direnv files (v1's .env* rule covered these), and local editor settings
*.pem
.envrc
.vscode/

# Debug logs
npm-debug.log*
